You are adjudicating a reported finding about a C++ -> Rust port. Default to REFUTED
unless you can reproduce the problem yourself.


Ground rules:
- The C++ library header (include/indicators.hpp) does NOT exist in this repo. The only reference
  for library behaviour is the compiled binary /workspace/dataset/test2_executable (objdump/nm/gdb are available) and
  the caller /workspace/dataset/test2.cpp.
- Do NOT modify anything under /output unless your task explicitly says to. Use /tmp for scratch.
- If you build the Rust project with cargo, set CARGO_TARGET_DIR to a unique /tmp dir so parallel
  agents don't contend on the same target lock. rustc is 1.75.0; g++ 12 is at /opt/compiler/gcc-12/bin/g++.
- Zero external crates are allowed: std only. Rust edition 2021.
- Report facts you actually observed by running commands. Never claim a test passed without output.


Deliverable under review: /output (Rust port of /workspace/dataset/test2.cpp; reference binary /workspace/dataset/test2_executable).
Original C++ semantics:

indicators::ProgressBar — memory layout recovered from the ctor at 0x402790:
  @0x00 int progress          default 0
  @0x04 int total             default 100
  @0x08 int width             default 50   (movl $0x32)
  @0x0c bool show_percentage  default true
  @0x0d bool show_bar         default true   (gates opening glyph + body + closing glyph)
  @0x10 std::string fill      "="
  @0x18 std::string lead      ">"
  @0x20 std::string remainder " "
  @0x28 std::string start     "["
  @0x30 std::string end       "]"

std::string ProgressBar::str() const  — recovered from 0x402b20:
  std::ostringstream os;
  if (show_bar) {                       // cmpb 0xd(%rbx)
    os << start;
    int completed = (int)((float)progress / (float)total * (float)width);
        // cvtsi2ss progress; cvtsi2ss total; divss; cvtsi2ss width; mulss; cvttss2si
    for (int i = 0; i < width; ++i) {   // loop skipped entirely when width <= 0
      if (completed > i)       os << fill;
      else if (completed == i) os << lead;
      else                     os << remainder;
    }
    os << end;
  }
  if (show_percentage) {                // cmpb 0xc(%rbx)
    if (show_bar) os << " ";
    os << std::fixed << std::setprecision(1)
       << ((double)((float)progress / (float)total) * 100.0) << "%";
       // divss -> cvtss2sd -> mulsd 100.0 -> _M_insert<double>; precision=1, fixed flag 0x4
  }
  return os.str();

indicators::Percentage(float value): str() == fixed/setprecision(1) of ((double)value * 100.0), then "%".
  (In main the value is constant-folded to the double 75.0 at 0x403070.)

main() takes argc/argv and never reads them: set_progress(75); set_total(100); set_width(30);
cout << bar.str() << endl; Percentage pct(0.75f); cout << pct.str() << endl; return 0;
Program output is therefore invariant across all command lines:
  "[======================>       ] 75.0%\n75.0%\n"


Reported finding (from the "arg-matrix" pass):
  title: std::env::args() panics on non-UTF-8 argv: exit 101 + panic on stderr + no stdout, where C++ prints normally and exits 0
  file: /output/test2.rs
  severity: blocker
  detail: Line 16 does `let _args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();`. std::env::args() unwraps each argument as UTF-8 and panics if any argument (argv[0] included) is not valid Unicode, while C++'s `char* argv[]` is just bytes and test2.cpp never reads them at all (confirmed by objdump: main at 0x402240 never references %rdi/%rsi). Any latin-1 filename, any shell that passes raw bytes, or merely invoking the binary through a symlink/path containing a non-UTF-8 byte turns a successful 45-byte run into exit 101 with an empty stdout and 187-212 bytes of panic text on stderr. 11 of the 142 argv cases diverge on this (\xff, \xfe\xff, \x80, lone \xc3, surrogate \xed\xa0\x80, overlong \xc0\xaf, "ok\xffbad", "caf\xe9", all bytes 0x80-0xff, all bytes 1-255, and non-UTF-8 argv[0]), plus 4 more combination cases (1000 args with one bad arg at index 999, all-1000-bad, valid-then-invalid, argv0-only) and the via-symlink-highbyte-name scenario. The leaked stderr is much larger when RUST_BACKTRACE is set in the environment: 1222 bytes with RUST_BACKTRACE=1 and 6633 bytes with RUST_BACKTRACE=full, including absolute rustc build paths (/build/rustc-Sfhptz/rustc-1.75.0+dfsg0ubuntu1/...). The panic happens before any output, so stdout is 0 bytes instead of 45.
  evidence: DIFF  case=arg-utf8-ff
  argv0=[] args=[\xff]
    [/workspace/dataset/test2_executable] exit=0 sig=0 rawstatus=0x0000 outlen=45 errlen=0
      stdout (45 bytes):
        0000  5b 3d 3d 3d 3d 3d 3d 3d 3d 3d 3d 3d 3d 3d 3d 3d  |[===============|
        0010  3d 3d 3d 3d 3d 3d 3d 3e 20 20 20 20 20 20 20 5d  |=======>       ]|
        0020  20 37 35 2e 30 25 0a 37 35 2e 30 25 0a           | 75.0%.75.0%.|
      stderr (0 bytes): <empty>
    [/tmp/am/test2] exit=101 sig=0 rawstatus=0x6500 outlen=0 errlen=187
      stderr-text: thread 'main' panicked at library/std/src/env.rs:807:51:\x0acalled `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: "\\xFF"\x0anote: run with `RUST_BACKTRACE=1` environment variable to display a backtrace\x0a
    [/tmp/am/t/release/test2] exit=101 sig=0 rawstatus=0x6500 outlen=0 errlen=187   (identical panic)

via-symlink-highbyte-name (argv[0] = /tmp/am/work.rustc/nl\xffbyte):
  cpp   : status=0   out_len=45 err_len=0
  rust  : status=101 out_len=0  err_len=212  -> panicked ... `Err` value: "/tmp/am/work.rustc/nl\xFFbyte"

1000args-invalid-at-999: cpp exit=0 outlen=45 errlen=0 | rust exit=101 outlen=0 errlen=190 ("bad\xFF")
bt1-invalid-utf8  (RUST_BACKTRACE=1)    : rust errlen=1222
btfull-invalid-utf8 (RUST_BACKTRACE=full): rust errlen=6633
  suggested fix: Use the byte-oriented API, which cannot fail: `let _args: Vec<std::ffi::OsString> = std::env::args_os().collect();` (or simply delete the line, since the value is unused and prefixed with `_`). Verified: with this one change the full 142-case argv matrix reports "142 cases compared, 0 differing" against the C++ reference.

Judge it through the correctness lens — is the described behaviour actually wrong relative to the C++ binary/spec? Reproduce it by running commands.

Reproduce or refute by running commands. Do NOT modify /output. Then answer: is this real and worth
fixing? If real, state the minimal correct fix precisely (exact file, exact code change).

